#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "89d0136d-63d1-540b-a3be-acff2d0bbbb5" :question/math-academy-id "e-11027" :question/problem "For the function  $g(x,y,z) = 2 (x + 1)e^z+ yz,$  evaluate  $\\dfrac{\\partial g}{\\partial z}$ at $(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z}+yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2(x+1){e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1)\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & =2(x+1){e}^{z}+y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂z}{|}_{(1,1,1)} & =2(1+1){e}^{1}+1 \\\\  & =4e+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "0fd784a0-5146-5f7b-9936-8d3bc43d7062" :question/math-academy-id "e-11028" :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ for $f(x,y) =x^2y\\ln{x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) & =y\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)) \\\\  & =y\\cdot ((2x)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x})) \\\\  & =y(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x) \\\\  & =xy(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192514 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "fc8ba431-5a97-5f57-9a83-fdcf4ac98d39" :question/math-academy-id "e-11048" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) =\\ln(y-x^2),$ evaluate $f_x(1,9).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(y-{x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y-{x}^{2}}\\cdot (y\\cdot 0-2x) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{y-{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,9)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,9) & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{9-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/id #uuid "6f7bdbcf-fb8e-5c70-b368-4e73cc94bdb2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11112" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{4x-y}{x+y^2},$ evaluate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{4x-y}{x+{y}^{2}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{2})}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (x+{y}^{2})-(4x-y)\\cdot 1}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x+4{y}^{2}-4x+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{y}^{2}+y}{{(x+{y}^{2})}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,-1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(2,-1)} & =\\frac{4(-1)^{2}+(-1)}{{(2+{(-1)}^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1152fe53-6276-5ced-85b3-1185032862f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x{z}^{2}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566397} {:answer/id #uuid "309e17aa-61ae-54a8-8688-b433b95eac50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566459} {:answer/id #uuid "342562b9-a910-5c9b-8120-d05d2a2c5abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566464} {:answer/id #uuid "ba79e0ef-a3a4-5a04-b1f2-586831978e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566734} {:answer/id #uuid "d4bdccd4-86bb-5a36-b660-24e6958bf711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "342562b9-a910-5c9b-8120-d05d2a2c5abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566464} :answer-field/id #uuid "c69bcd40-ed79-55be-8b7b-cb7c0491af3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a6bdefd5-aef3-5d16-afc3-d28fde40b19b" :question/math-academy-id "q-132245" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ for $f(x,y,z)={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the chain rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y-x{z}^{4}) & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4y)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{4})) \\\\  & ={e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot (0-x\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{4})) \\\\  & =-{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\cdot x\\cdot 4{z}^{3} \\\\  & =-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-4x{z}^{3}{e}^{4y-x{z}^{4}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3687686f-27d7-5790-b17c-25679b2d1bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566472} {:answer/id #uuid "98ba232a-4707-557a-b5e6-2e7896a3faba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{x-1}-3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566662} {:answer/id #uuid "b619f3ad-5474-53a1-863a-0d77cd05c096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{x-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566721} {:answer/id #uuid "b787c94d-cc66-56fe-ab2f-bae4827c49ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3y{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566727} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c9bfa-7951-5e10-bace-6cd8713f434a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3687686f-27d7-5790-b17c-25679b2d1bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566472} :answer-field/id #uuid "31a1c922-50b2-51e3-a611-69b79808fdf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "059319c8-1dff-5dfe-8695-5074282cdfe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-132248" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y,z)$ for $f(x,y,z)={y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x}-y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{z}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{x})-{z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =x{y}^{x-1}-{z}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4008d421-bcc4-541e-b6d1-6f911e6c8d66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2x\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276034} {:answer/id #uuid "430b88eb-d781-5955-bdd4-cba096425762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276071} {:answer/id #uuid "4fccdda9-5259-5b0d-9374-c4a4d5a9b18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276193} {:answer/id #uuid "ba43033d-0504-5559-9fb4-0a81d932ee52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277130} {:answer/id #uuid "f142f4cb-083a-5c32-9524-ba93f1707e6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277595}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "430b88eb-d781-5955-bdd4-cba096425762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276071} :answer-field/id #uuid "05705cd0-c421-54fa-8117-fe18fa6f3bc3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0833bc78-75ed-516b-b7f3-7df11e162117" :question/math-academy-id "q-159658" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{2xy-1}) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-1) & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}(2x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\cdot (2x\\cdot 1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{\\sqrt{2xy-1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df86f71-4e8c-5223-a2c3-114b3c3a90ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566425} {:answer/id #uuid "23ed9481-6f88-5b6b-806c-7902c0dee17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} {:answer/id #uuid "755654b4-0e0c-54a7-a879-4314f25dc630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566584} {:answer/id #uuid "79a314e6-0668-5c44-b3d1-02a3cf9ad5c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566595} {:answer/id #uuid "7b9ccd66-db58-59f7-af47-1c86f73f9c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23ed9481-6f88-5b6b-806c-7902c0dee17a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566439} :answer-field/id #uuid "789cacb9-1990-5456-9318-5cf3591eaa13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566934}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "738ade6c-0972-56b4-b1bb-9f8fdeb237e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-159661" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y},$ evaluate ${f}_{y}(3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y}+{x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{x+y})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))+{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}\\cdot (0+1)+{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+y}}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(3,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(3,1) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3+1}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253445fe-fff1-58b7-9019-3e38491dac58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566442} {:answer/id #uuid "2ddd0cfc-1ccf-5d4e-857f-c5a638f03f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566455} {:answer/id #uuid "72a3ae63-0575-5426-a7c0-15120c576ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566577} {:answer/id #uuid "87430684-ea2a-5175-8c38-072537596c8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566619} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6a36f-25e4-5d5d-aa20-c816606c275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566686}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5a6a36f-25e4-5d5d-aa20-c816606c275d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566686} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e6bb6c0-dbeb-5577-8434-7c53a76a2f69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dfe77a47-89cf-58c9-a7e4-db0e7d6a133d" :question/math-academy-id "q-159662" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ evaluate ${f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the sum and constant multiple rules for differenciation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\cdot (2x+{y}^{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,\\sqrt{3}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1+3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "139d6455-ad86-5abf-8c88-8199b32eebe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566400} {:answer/id #uuid "730f6e85-bd23-53e6-ab08-4141d676fa5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566580} {:answer/id #uuid "a029f170-0196-57bc-a811-b716dfabb716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566674} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ea4bae-10d8-5476-97e9-6ae33a493dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566798} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b3f484-b92d-53c5-8123-dbca1cc7dffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8ea4bae-10d8-5476-97e9-6ae33a493dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566798} :answer-field/id #uuid "716e7851-801e-59a3-b80e-fe5f30999db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7285b21-99ee-5b47-b1b3-ef79da9b3c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159698" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)) & =(3{x}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)+{x}^{3}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy))\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-{x}^{3}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "124ba60a-ec47-5dd9-97fc-d33ccf7d0e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275645} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa03d48-f644-5066-b304-d3fc3f4f62eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276292} {:answer/id #uuid "5c6c70be-1878-50b5-989f-a8ed0e5f587f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276303} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b747a7-3b88-5c3b-b25b-53403f09d052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276925} {:answer/id #uuid "ec320ce3-f38d-5b16-8a80-a7b57185a46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277549}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "124ba60a-ec47-5dd9-97fc-d33ccf7d0e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275645} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d4deb5e-7cf6-55ae-b93a-f09121cdee49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "95a36042-f58f-5fde-b6a7-7ca45f1d6809" :question/math-academy-id "q-159700" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x+y}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{sin}^{2}⁡y} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(x+y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{{sin}^{2}⁡y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,\\pi /2)} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-(\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381258 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "764fe799-f9d4-56fc-8786-768351e90b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381260} {:answer/id #uuid "af40a1cc-fcdd-5d82-88f1-afb1cb810dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381261} {:answer/id #uuid "238d2bb5-2057-5938-92cb-490c8fd1d052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381262} {:answer/id #uuid "007df772-538e-5628-b388-eb1493c3de4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381263} {:answer/id #uuid "e31f346f-c25e-5e5e-9897-5f3acda1e293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381264}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af40a1cc-fcdd-5d82-88f1-afb1cb810dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381261} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cf4f0d-d3ae-5f4a-af95-43db53f9c0fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381259}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba29a24e-25aa-5034-8849-e753260af75f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159706" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(\\pi ,-\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(\\pi ,-\\pi )} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1)(-1)}{(-1-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186381265 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8367eed8-8db9-5b16-8030-4fb1d45343f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381267} {:answer/id #uuid "c3f7d926-4b2e-5a47-a964-89bbf3409d2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{e}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381268} {:answer/id #uuid "c0684d01-ce2e-5d72-b7c5-5083711bc071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381269} {:answer/id #uuid "3e29fb6f-8142-5709-a889-a0d7208a0ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381270} {:answer/id #uuid "20723cc1-5581-5f10-b006-0cc5888b60c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381271}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8367eed8-8db9-5b16-8030-4fb1d45343f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381267} :answer-field/id #uuid "c43d33af-7b21-5589-9d8f-5fccd66d3600" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186381266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f6e1b67-30d1-5d26-980f-cea342aa0dc8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159707" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the quotient and chain rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}) & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y-x})}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x\\cdot {e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-x)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x{e}^{y-x}-({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot {e}^{y-x}\\cdot (-1)}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}){e}^{y-x}}{{({e}^{y-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2x+{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{e}^{y-x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,-1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,-1)} & =\\frac{2(1)+{1}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{e}^{-1-1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+1-1}{{e}^{-2}} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567047 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bae9d67-b69f-5f47-b67a-7079c02affc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566388} {:answer/id #uuid "0f14b9cf-9b15-5156-b4dd-56a81035a051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2y{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566392} {:answer/id #uuid "44ca59e7-cf5e-53c0-b7ed-60b5a8af0113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566497} {:answer/id #uuid "8e59a02a-bdaa-55fc-af47-354a579ea6d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡(2y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566638} {:answer/id #uuid "d51bdaab-f954-5323-81d6-75ecb0d92637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+x{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44ca59e7-cf5e-53c0-b7ed-60b5a8af0113" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566497} :answer-field/id #uuid "809f59c6-6dda-58ee-9a9d-a816868161a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "67e10996-e62d-5489-9a64-73c5e56228d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-159710" :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the constant multiple rule and the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x((1)\\cdot \\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy\\cdot {sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+xy{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{tan}⁡({y}^{2})+2x{y}^{2}{sec}^{2}⁡({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60243795-2a92-591c-983d-ab0a12df51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} {:answer/id #uuid "7ccef17a-7e81-5ad2-b543-a2a7ee459876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-y)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566601} {:answer/id #uuid "b662e7db-0fcb-5572-a49f-f705511b1c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{csc}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566724} {:answer/id #uuid "d3f81ca3-bfba-5c9f-b61e-29a424ecf9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566781} {:answer/id #uuid "e65931e8-e876-5d62-8009-c6a3aab5a425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60243795-2a92-591c-983d-ab0a12df51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566544} :answer-field/id #uuid "a55eaa90-3c66-5bf4-a7e7-2ac1f5a2db6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5d644ebc-b214-5b17-9daa-3ced5a3257b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159712" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient and chain rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y))\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y})}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{x+y}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)\\cdot {e}^{x+y}\\cdot 1}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)){e}^{x+y}}{{({e}^{x+y})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y)}{{e}^{x+y}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x-y).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f76324-5a68-5230-a5cd-bf08feffd775" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275530} {:answer/id #uuid "89f30304-64a1-56ba-bd73-bcc48e23b10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276724} {:answer/id #uuid "9562c48d-8b5b-53a4-9421-210d904f36b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276823} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ad9708-221f-5829-9e8f-6b54521f34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276941} {:answer/id #uuid "dd43b247-ee16-5db2-b7b2-06fdc79123a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ad9708-221f-5829-9e8f-6b54521f34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276941} :answer-field/id #uuid "d372165d-773c-522b-a6ae-4635a798bcf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c4ebeea-f58f-5eb8-93aa-37b6d0eeb259" :question/math-academy-id "q-159716" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x))-{y}^{3}\\cdot (1) \\\\  & =((4{x}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{x}^{4}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x))-{y}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Quotient Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0163c90f-63f7-520c-b90b-cdca85bc5861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566364} {:answer/id #uuid "78d2aa47-e366-536d-979b-7aaddb43183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} {:answer/id #uuid "90f591ac-d503-53e4-9edf-df0ecead1c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566647} {:answer/id #uuid "ae8aa054-536b-5175-8e3d-21f8ecd92148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566710} {:answer/id #uuid "c62a4f9c-30eb-5f65-9868-b6c9578e4d31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6y-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78d2aa47-e366-536d-979b-7aaddb43183d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566591} :answer-field/id #uuid "5af82be6-e454-517f-89c6-df6d183337b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1691ed28-0c5c-54a8-9012-9dfc00861f65" :question/math-academy-id "q-159719" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}.$ The partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{h(x,y)}{(7y-6x)^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nUsing the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{3y-2x}{7y-6x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y-2x)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7y-6x)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)\\cdot (7y-6x)-(3y-2x)\\cdot (-6)}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-14y+12x+18y-12x}{(7y-6x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4y}{(7y-6x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nh(x,y)=4y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5dca404c-a5ec-59b9-86f2-7bef312a3a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276314} {:answer/id #uuid "60f31fe4-d889-5663-b09c-1f3d3a934e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276351} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d9bb43-7bc8-5503-9cb9-8fa76c5d61b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277059} {:answer/id #uuid "cf8085bd-299e-5a6d-b588-c8dc819bbb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277313} {:answer/id #uuid "dd32ec93-9bb3-5504-9d2b-d507b1f2c7fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277413}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60f31fe4-d889-5663-b09c-1f3d3a934e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276351} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c4fa2ab-2f5d-570f-a779-b76e2b67ca98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d20c624a-1709-5495-88ea-45d0df97ea34" :question/math-academy-id "q-159751" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}))-{x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot 1)-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}-2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1)} & ={e}^{1\\cdot 1}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 1}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =e+e-2 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192785 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e960f6d1-5006-5ddc-ada2-59b46924bf73" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Product Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a78e326-4c51-50ac-aad3-5c443d334667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275864} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbfe750-2540-5243-bc59-e0f79dc31902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276300} {:answer/id #uuid "6a035055-68f4-5be4-adae-4803c1279845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276429} {:answer/id #uuid "8b99f649-3f43-5a3f-8b56-e0ddedb38c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3+\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276746} {:answer/id #uuid "9ca8f984-ab7a-5d4f-91f6-049dec25f1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a78e326-4c51-50ac-aad3-5c443d334667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275864} :answer-field/id #uuid "f21cf5ea-df9a-5241-8868-5373b87209ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7a86e6a-207f-552d-b9da-b1e5c673da4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-159768" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)=xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(2,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) & =x(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =x(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(2,\\pi ):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(2,\\pi )} & =2\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1-\\pi \\cdot 0)+(-1) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6537a211-988c-5668-b744-a7a835445a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321932} {:answer/id #uuid "71e4806a-8e4b-520c-928b-81dbadc1fe14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322044} {:answer/id #uuid "960d1e29-7934-50e7-a295-0f743083eb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322339} {:answer/id #uuid "b481fb26-41eb-5d5c-b42b-d663d5f13d9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322557} {:answer/id #uuid "def87247-a78e-5ef4-8eed-60df8bc36b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "960d1e29-7934-50e7-a295-0f743083eb53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322339} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e6e9ad1-12b8-512c-a42e-c331613a9a75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323292}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3cd9c82e-fba1-518a-8285-94d9c7befa44" :question/math-academy-id "q-35710" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-(0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2(1)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323711 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "359742a6-8a0e-5705-b305-bdbfab09ad97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321557} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e8e21f-dd05-54d4-9135-759212c11a07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322582} {:answer/id #uuid "baf21348-aa27-59ef-b997-7967d7ce27a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y(x+1)^{2y-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322597} {:answer/id #uuid "c728a1b6-53de-52b8-9756-83f9687e51da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691} {:answer/id #uuid "d6641433-9b61-5746-a0f6-b80a7538be32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c728a1b6-53de-52b8-9756-83f9687e51da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e72f75b7-2611-5c69-b6cd-8d8554967419" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323624}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9932dc44-faa7-51c4-b14b-b1d882efadd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-35713" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ for $f(x,y)=(x+1)^{{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the chain rule: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((x+1)^{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+1) & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\cdot (1+0) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}(x+1)^{{y}^{2}-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37ea7ae-4cef-5fcd-9228-e2df831da5fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382126} {:answer/id #uuid "679825cc-7f11-5343-ae88-84a1ca97e167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382127} {:answer/id #uuid "0df78ffc-b050-5411-91a2-dfd4d4406f7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382128} {:answer/id #uuid "3cea194b-2a28-5191-a2e3-0a7f014011fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382129} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7f3664-511a-5234-bd52-b3ad5619722f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "679825cc-7f11-5343-ae88-84a1ca97e167" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382127} :answer-field/id #uuid "96d9e209-65c0-591c-9c57-813c74680ed7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "684028b0-fca2-5e81-bf94-98e01d24397f" :question/math-academy-id "q-64906" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}+{e}^{xy}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\cdot 3{x}^{2}+{e}^{xy}\\cdot y\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+y{e}^{xy}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(-1,1)} & =3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}+(1){e}^{(-1)(1)} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186382152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac4949ac-c2c5-5400-9b81-caf9da897244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382154} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb86e61-8965-50a0-9c65-7710e3f23e54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382155} {:answer/id #uuid "ca85cdde-ed68-5dfd-a8df-8739efb9ad70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382156} {:answer/id #uuid "360dc3a3-e4a5-57c2-9e1c-028e7af0458b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382157} {:answer/id #uuid "493e03c2-21cb-58c1-95b8-a3d64817cab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac4949ac-c2c5-5400-9b81-caf9da897244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382154} :answer-field/id #uuid "524bf164-94aa-5c0e-a84e-da7f0acbfdd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186382153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e55c37e1-12b0-54db-abbc-4811c1a7e4c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-70030" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂x}$ at the point $(1,1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{3})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={z}^{3}\\cdot 1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & ={z}^{3}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}{|}_{(1,1,2)} & ={2}^{3}-2(1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (1)) \\\\  & =8-2(1)(-1) \\\\  & =8+2 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192784 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b5cebc16-84c3-5ec8-b1f1-5f9c51a69d94" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f1b3428-f0ff-5ea9-ae99-bf8657ba7b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352412} {:answer/id #uuid "689b5ddb-7080-5cde-993b-3f0536812305" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352500} {:answer/id #uuid "7cdee458-66d1-5075-a5b3-b90c8837d4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352664} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7a10a6-a680-5c5d-bddf-8913d3cf78ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353158} {:answer/id #uuid "d4fc7604-4fda-5395-8026-a179d600a128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353415}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f1b3428-f0ff-5ea9-ae99-bf8657ba7b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352412} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7aa88dc-8c66-5720-9d75-ddad96eeb69d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce4a1a73-6331-5e40-b163-83a2f690e9c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86812" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}{e}^{xy}-{y}^{2}{e}^{yz},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂z}$ at the point $(0,-1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $z,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}{e}^{xy}-{y}^{2}{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂z}({y}^{2}{e}^{yz}) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{yz})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2})-{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{yz}) & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot 2z-{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{yz}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz) \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}\\cdot 2z-{y}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{yz}\\cdot y \\\\  & =2z{e}^{xy}-{y}^{3}{e}^{yz}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(0,-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂z}{|}_{(0,-1,1)} & =2(1){e}^{(0)(-1)}-(-1)^{3}{e}^{(-1)(1)} \\\\  & =2+{e}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
